<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n12378" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">dĕ-ascĭo</span></b> ,
			āvi, ātum, 1, v. a.
		1, ascio, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>to hew</i> or <i>cut with an axe, to
				smoothe</i>. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b> (late Lat.): deasciato stipiti, <i>wrought, smoothed</i>, Prud. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">στεφ</span>. 10, 381.— </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>B.</b>
			<i>To rub out, efface</i>: hunc titulum, Murat. Inscr.
			1203, 9.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>: aliquem, <i>to cheat, to
				chouse</i> (cf. abrado), <i><span class="en">Plaut. Mil.
				3, 3, 11</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
